# The DASHI integration / TOE spine

A single small spine that carries the corpus-wide cross-domain pass, rather than
a collection of per-domain reports.  Reading order:

1. `Integration.Levels` — the four-level classification (vocabulary, stored
   record dependency, shared implementation type, proved transport) as a Lean
   datatype, together with `Transport`, `NoTransport`, `Boundary` and `Socket`.
   Evidence constructors determine the level, so no row can be filed above the
   level it has a witness for.
2. `Integration.TernaryHub` — the corpus's six ternary carriers (`Trit`, the
   `Base369` tone, `Digit369`, the SSP trit carrier, the codec `TritCode`, the
   loom's `BTBranch`) are one object with one balanced coordinate, forced up to
   sign; every supplied polarity map becomes negation.
3. `Integration.Base369Group` — the 3/6/9 layer as finite cyclic groups: the
   loom's `projection2x3` realised as `ℤ/6 ≅ ℤ/2 × ℤ/3`, and the proof that the
   nonary layer is *not* two ternary layers.
4. `Integration.Address` — the prefix ultrametric on digit addresses, developed
   once for an arbitrary alphabet, with the codec-to-369 isometry.
5. `Integration.ClopenBalls` — supervoxel/clopen refinement: prefix balls are
   open and closed, refinement is exactly containment, no ball is atomic, and
   the corpus's clopen-authority record is a contentless socket.
6. `Integration.ReceiptSocket` — the archetypal receipt record and the exact
   sense in which it constrains nothing, plus the strengthened record that does.
7. `Integration.Refinement` — the corpus's never-instantiated refinement-system
   record, inhabited twice (dialectic loom, ternary addresses).
8. `Integration.MonsterFifteen` — the 15-prime mask ultrametric *is* the
   depth-15 prefix geometry, and the hyperfabric support map is a non-expansive
   but non-isometric, non-injective, non-homomorphic coarsening onto it.
9. `Integration.CognitionSupervoxel` — a cognition domain instance at the
   supervoxel junction: the phase-enriched trit, its constraint system, the
   proof that the "public zero" is not atomic (a `Bool` placeholder in the
   source), and the polarity-equivariance of the cognition observable.
10. `Integration.UltrametricCensus` — every live instance of the corpus's
   `Ultrametric` record is the same prefix geometry pulled back along a map,
   with one exception (the `Monster` walk distance) that provably is not.
11. `Integration.PhaseQuotient` — the corpus's nine-state carrier as the orbit
   space of a free `C₃` action on the 27 ternary endomorphism codes, its chart
   onto the `Base369` nonary labels, the *transported* exponent-three algebra
   those labels then carry, and the proof that the address carrier does not
   determine the algebra.  Also the two-origin dichotomy for `triXor`: additive
   in the native index chart, affine in the polarity-compatible balanced chart.
12. `Integration.SSPWeave` — the SSP orientation monoid, its `ℤ/2` quotient, and
   the functorial transport `T_{q∘p} = T_q ∘ T_p` on lane states, whose carrier
   is the ternary hub: the algebra the 15-lane layer does preserve.
13. `Integration.ObserverQuotient` — what the Boolean observer keeps (the
   symmetry action, uniquely descending) and what it forgets (the sign, and any
   binary law at all), plus the signed relational defect layer on which the
   preserved algebra lives.
14. `Integration.PolarResidual` — the residual-bearing 9 → 6 projection as an
   exact `9 ≃ 6 + 3` equivalence, with the impossibility of a direct retraction.
15. `Integration.ResidualQuotient` — the "coarse state + retained residual
   reopens the rich state" pattern as one structure, with the polar projection
   and the magnitude observer as proved instances, and one lemma explaining why
   the residual is never removable.
16. `Integration.SymmetryReduction` — "symmetry reduction" as a typed
   obligation: `EquivariantQuotient` bundles an action on each layer with the
   commuting square `π (g · x) = ḡ · π x`.  Inhabited by the free `C₃`
   27 → 9 phase reduction, by the polarity/magnitude observer, and by one
   instance with nontrivial induced action; and the native-chart support
   observer is shown to admit no such structure at all.
17. `Integration.GaugeReduction`, `Integration.EquivariantChannel`,
   `Integration.GaugeObserver` — "symmetry reduction" split into its two
   genuinely different cases.  Quotienting by the *whole* group forces the
   induced action to be trivial (`coarse_action_trivial_of_orbit`); a
   `GaugeReduction` for a subgroup `H` of `G` with quotient `Q = G/H` instead
   removes a redundancy and leaves `Q` acting, with `redundant_iff_gauge`
   showing the invisible symmetries are exactly `H`.  `EqObserverBuild` supplies
   the transverse channel in that case, where the surviving symmetry moves every
   class, and `gauge_welded_theorem` states all the conclusions at once.
18. `Integration.GaugeSU2`, `Integration.GaugeSSP` — the two instances: the
   finite axis-lift lane with the deck `C₂` quotiented and the axis rotation
   `C₃` surviving, and the SSP nine-cell lane with the depth wheel `C₃`
   quotiented and the polarity flip `C₂` surviving.  Each carries the positive
   transverse certificate.  No claim that these finite carriers are Lie groups,
   and no analytic content, is made.
19. `Integration.DescentLevel` — towers of observers and the *coarsest*
   observation at which an invariant is still a function of the observed state,
   proved unique and computed on the axis lane (`axis_coarsest_sufficient`).
20. `Integration.EnumInvariance` — the certificate is presentation free: any two
   enumerations of the carrier give the same number, because the mean is
   permutation invariant and the transverse coordinates are merely permuted.
   `det_gram_reindex` is the family-level counterpart.
21. `Integration.CertificateFormula` — the certificate in closed form: it equals
   `(n+3)(m − m²/n) − (1 − m/n)²` for a carrier of size `n` and a class of size
   `m`, so it distinguishes exactly those states whose classes differ in size.
22. `Integration.SSPThreshold` — the SSP lane's observational threshold: the
   gauge weld's own channel gives a constant certificate and no threshold, the
   zero-detecting channel gives the two values `212/9`, `215/9` and the
   threshold `k = 1`.
23. `Integration.GaugePreimage` — composite gauge redundancy promoted to
   subgroup data: the preimage subgroup `quot⁻¹(K)`, its normality, and the
   composition law `(G/H)/K ≅ G/quot⁻¹(K)` in the form
   `compGaugeQuotient_exact`, with `compGaugeExact_redundant_iff` identifying
   the invisible symmetries with exactly that subgroup.
24. `Integration.LeastSufficient` — sufficiency and refinement: the invariant is
   the least informative sufficient observer, the tower notion is that notion
   level by level, and a bounded-horizon variant separates "last sufficient
   layer before loss" from "coarsest sufficient observation available".
25. `Integration.OrbitResidual` — how much a symmetry quotient must hide: the
   residual is injective on fibres, hence has at least `[G : Stab x]` states.
26. `Integration.CertificatePays` — when a positive certificate controls
   something: a certificate budget is a defect budget, with the honest negative
   counterpart that a bounded weighted aggregate does not bound a local defect.
27. `Integration.ApproximateIntertwiner` — exact, certified-approximate and
   unverified squares kept apart, with additive error under composition and
   iteration.
28. `Integration.StructureMorphism` — the single owner of the commuting square.
   `Exact` (definitionally the intertwiner), the exact/approximate/unverified
   tiering, the transport an exact square buys (trajectories, fixed points,
   finite reachability, with the honest forward-only gate), the `Sound`
   quotient-transport tier with `sound_iff_factors`, the group-indexed `Gauge`
   tier, and `structure_morphism_unification` exhibiting the
   representation-compatibility, intertwiner, quotient-soundness and
   gauge-reduction lanes as instances of one predicate.
29. `Integration.PhaseGaugeU1` — the first non-finite gauge instance: a global
   `U(1)` phase on a complex inner product space.  The norm and the whole
   Hermitian form are invariant (so any Gram-built certificate is), the state
   and any linear channel are equivariant, the raw phase coordinate is
   sensitive; the modulus is a *complete* invariant of the orbit; modulus plus
   retained phase reopens the state and the residual is not removable; and the
   whole thing is a `GaugeReduction` with an exact gauge datum.
30. `Integration.CertifiedMargin` — "certificate beats defect" as one theorem:
   the margin `G ≥ g, B ≤ b, b < g ⟹ G − B > 0`, the dimensionless relative
   defect with its contraction gate and its architecture-level no-go, the two
   asymptotic regimes, compensation before estimation (a reflection symmetry
   forces the first order at the reference to vanish, so the compensated defect
   is of strictly higher order), and the proof that a source-normalisation
   coefficient can move one and the same structural inequality across the gate.
31. `Integration.MultiscaleDescent` — law descent through a tower of
   coarsenings: descent at a level *is* the exact square, descent composes and
   carries trajectories, errors add along the tower, and the strongest negative
   `NoEffectiveLaw` (no coarse operator whatsoever closes the square) is
   realised on a three-level instance together with a `1/2` lower bound on the
   defect of every candidate effective law.
32. `Integration.CommutatorObservability` — fine noncommutativity with coarse
   flatness: an observer can erase path order.  The generic pattern, the
   positive control showing coarse flatness alone is cheap, an explicit
   four-state carrier realising both halves, and the *coarsest sufficient
   observation* for the commutator defect on that carrier, computed exactly.
33. `Integration.PathTransport` — the shared path substrate: paths as the
   reflexive-transitive closure of a step relation, transport along a
   simulation, the deterministic case identified with `Reaches`, the **monotone
   obstruction** to reverse reachability, and the AND/OR (hypergraph) proof
   system as a specialisation whose provability transports along a simulation.
34. `Integration.ResidualTaxonomy` — five typed classes of residual
   (reconstructive, trajectory, contrastive, ambiguity, combinatorial), each
   with the law that distinguishes it, plus the separations showing they are not
   one type with five names.
35. `Integration.EquivarianceObstruction` — an equivariant equivalence forces
   fixed-point sets, stabilizers, orbits and the period spectrum to agree, so a
   free action is never equivariantly equivalent to one with a fixed point.  The
   instance uses two three-element carriers, making the point that the
   obstruction is orbit structure and not cardinality.
36. `Integration.LocalRealisation` — semantic reduction is not computational
   locality: plans with identical semantics have unbounded exposure, so a
   locality certificate is separate data and cannot be manufactured from
   extensional agreement.
37. `Integration.FieldControlledDynamics` — a fixed substrate with a background
   field: same state and different field give different successors, the same
   structural edge is effective under one field and not the other, and
   reachability inherits the dependence.  Plus the attractor vocabulary (fixed
   points, basins, cycles), eventual periodicity on a finite carrier, and three
   qualitative attractor classes on one carrier.
38. `Integration.ObserverBundle` — base, fibre, transport and measurement: the
   measurement square as the exact square of the owner, the failure case where
   no downstream transport closes it, and the measured pseudodistance, which is
   zero on distinct states whenever the measurement collides.
39. `Integration.ContrastBoundary` — increments as a discrete derivative (they
   telescope, and the present state does not determine them), and two-boundary
   conditioning, where the admissible intermediate set depends on both
   boundaries and is a function of neither alone.
40. `Integration.ConsumerGauge` — the three classes of readout of a symmetric
   state (invariant, equivariant, gauge-sensitive), with the orbit-quotient
   universal property, and the three classes realised simultaneously on the
   nine-cell SSP lane.
41. `Integration.Registry` — the checked global registry: positive rows with
   their evidence, negative rows with their refutations, sockets with their
   underdetermination proofs.
42. `Integration.RiemannReflection`, `Integration.RiemannStrip`,
   `Integration.RiemannSubstrate`, `Integration.RiemannXiSymmetry` — the RH/zeta
   *analytic* lane brought into the
   same vocabulary: the lane's reflection and three-phase carriers as hub
   transports, the critical line as the fixed locus of the lane's involution
   with the displacement as the kernel's transverse coordinate, and the analytic
