   `Integration.Experiment.ThermalRC`, the portability test of the continuous
   lane on a *nonlinearly* parameterised relaxation model.
51a. `Integration.Experiment.BestReading` — the continuous-lane measurement
   *choice*: a selector over a finite candidate set, proved to return one of the
   offered readings, to attain the maximal declared parameter sensitivity, and
   to contract the surviving region (and hence both envelopes) at least as much
   as every candidate — with the negative that equal sensitivities make the
   choice vacuous.
51b. `Integration.Experiment.ProbabilityLayer` — the separate probabilistic
   enrichment: finite probability mass functions with expectation, variance and
   covariance (Cauchy–Schwarz included); an additive measurement-noise model
   with a likelihood, proved to *refine* the declared-tolerance layer
   (`positive likelihood ⇒ tolerance-compatible`, strictly); and three negative
   results keeping `prediction envelope ≠ confidence interval ≠ credible
   interval` unerasable.  Nothing in the set-valued Stage-3/Stage-4 core imports
   this file, so no earlier result acquires a probabilistic reading.
51c. `Integration.Experiment.BayesUpdate` — Bayes' rule over a finite hypothesis
   family (`evidence`, `posterior` returned as a genuine `FinPMF`), with the
   support identity, the no-resurrection lemma, the division-free Bayes factor,
   `sequential_is_batch`, the flat-likelihood and total-refutation negatives,
   and the bridge to the set-valued core: with noise bounded by each reading's
   declared tolerance the posterior's support is contained in the surviving
   parameter region `Θ_E` (`posterior_support_subset_compatible`), a falsified
   family has zero evidence, and two negatives show the inclusion is strict and
   that a region determines no weights.
51d. `Integration.Experiment.MultiParameterRegion` — the surviving-region theory
   in several parameters: for a family affine in the parameter one reading cuts
   a convex closed slab, so the surviving region is convex and closed; a
   continuous consumer over a connected region has an interval envelope, and
   over a nonempty compact connected region the envelope is exactly `[min, max]`
   with both ends attained.  The identifiability dichotomy is the genuinely
   multi-dimensional content: a direction invisible to the readings is never
   resolved, fewer readings than parameters always leave such a direction (a
   rank obstruction, not a matter of tolerance size), and jointly injective
   gradients read exactly do pin the parameter down.  A two-parameter line
   instance shows both sides are satisfiable.
51e. `Integration.Experiment.CertifiedPropagation` — the certificate on a
   quantity-graph edge and the compiler field that consumes it: `CertifiedLaw`
   bundles an edge with a real extension of its rational evaluation rule and the
   proof that the edge's *own* declared sensitivity list is the vector of
   partial derivatives of that extension (discharged for `Q = I t` and
   `E = V Q`); `compile` now reports a `propagatedVariance`, proved to be
   `Cov.push` of the declared row against the diagonal input covariance,
   nonnegative, zero exactly when nothing is declared, and built from genuine
   derivatives when a certificate supplies the row.  Two negatives: the compiler
   cannot detect a wrong row, and a certified derivative is still only first
   order.
51f. `Integration.Experiment.NonlinearDiscrimination` — measurement choice
   beyond the affine case: a *separation modulus* (predictions separate the
   parameter at least linearly) confines the surviving region to an interval of
   half-width `tol / L`; an affine family has modulus `|slope|`, so the earlier
   result is the special case; the Stage-4 selector is reused to pick the
   candidate of maximal declared modulus, which gives the smallest guaranteed
   radius offered.  Two negatives keep the guarantee from being read as an
   ordering: a larger modulus does not imply a smaller surviving region (a
   modulus is a lower bound), and at modulus zero the guarantee is vacuous.
51g. `Integration.Experiment.CoverageSemantics` — Stage 6, lane 1: frequentist
   coverage.  A sampling model `θ ↦ law θ`, a set-valued procedure and the
   coverage probability `P_θ(θ ∈ C(Y))`; the confidence statement `HasLevel`;
   Bonferroni composition of two certified procedures; and the bridge — under
   noise bounded by the declared tolerance the Stage-4 admissible region is a
   coverage-one procedure.  Four negatives keep prediction envelope, credible
   region and confidence region distinct, including that no function of the
   reported set returns a level.
51h. `Integration.Experiment.ContinuousBayes` — Stage 6, lane 2: Bayes over a
   continuous parameter.  A prior density on a compact interval, the evidence
   integral, the posterior density (proved to integrate to one), the support
   identity, the division-free Bayes ratio, closure of the structure under
   updating with `sequential_is_batch`, and the bridge: the support of the
   posterior density lies inside the surviving parameter region.  The negative:
   two priors with the same support give different posteriors.
51i. `Integration.Experiment.VarianceBudget` — Stage 6, lane 3: the uncertainty
   budget derived from the graph.  The first-order propagated variance of a
   certified sensitivity row against an input covariance, its per-input
   contribution shares (complete for independent inputs), the dominant
   contributor, and measurement selection by actual uncertainty reduction.  Two
   negatives: share accounting ignores correlation, and no graph information
   supplies a model-discrepancy term.
51j. `Integration.Experiment.LocalIdentifiability` — Stage 6, lane 4: nonlinear
   local identifiability.  An injective strict derivative gives local injectivity
   of the measurement map; fewer measurement times than parameters force an
   invisible direction, for every model and at every operating point, and no
   tolerance repairs it.  Two negatives: the test is first-order only, and local
   identifiability is not global.
51k. `Integration.Experiment.TrajectorySensitivity` — Stage 6, lane 5: the
