51ab. `Integration.Variation.MetricDensity` — the first computational
   ingredient of the Einstein-side variation: Jacobi's formula
   `d/dt det(g + t h)|_0 = det g · tr(g⁻¹ h)` at the identity and at any
   invertible `g`, the volume-density variation
   `δ√(-g) = ½ √(-g) · g^{μν} δg_{μν}`, the fact that this variation is a
   linear trace pairing `h ↦ tr(Tᵀ h)` for one explicit `T`, and the negative
   that it annihilates a nonzero perturbation as soon as the index set has two
   elements — so the density factor alone never separates tensors.  No
   curvature, no manifold and no integration appear.
51ac. `Integration.Variation.SymmetricFibre` — the physical perturbation fibre.
   A metric perturbation is a *symmetric* 2-tensor, a proper subspace, so by
   item 51w's obstruction it cannot separate arbitrary tensors — and indeed
   the antisymmetric part of a tensor is invisible to it.  But symmetric
   perturbations *do* separate symmetric tensors, so the weld survives
   restriction to the physical fibre exactly when both sides are known to be
   symmetric: the symmetry of the stress tensor is load-bearing, not cosmetic.
51ad. `Integration.Variation.SymmetricField` — the symmetric perturbation
   *field* fibre.  A physical metric perturbation is one symmetric matrix per
   base point, paired against the stress through a density-weighted sum.  Point
   supported ("compactly supported") symmetric probes already separate symmetric
   stress fields, so the admissible physical fibre need not be all of field
   space; but a base point where the density vanishes is completely invisible,
   and without symmetry of the stress field the pointwise antisymmetric part is
   invisible as before.  So three hypotheses — symmetry, nonvanishing density
   and pointwise probes — are exactly what the field-level weld needs.
51ae. `Integration.Variation.InverseMetric` — the second algebraic ingredient
   of the Einstein–Hilbert variation: `δ(g⁻¹) = −g⁻¹ (δg) g⁻¹` at any invertible
   `g`, its trace-pairing form, the fact that it preserves symmetry and, unlike
   the density variation, loses no information about the perturbation; combined
   with item 51ab it gives `δ(√(-g) g⁻¹)`.  Still no curvature and no manifold.
51af. `Integration.Variation.SectorFilter` — sector aggregation beyond
